					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607946536" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;" >cybersqyd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607940057 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">14 Dec 2020 10:00</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4844414">
									

<p>I think my biggest issue with this is that you don't really do a lot with the concept: while 'thing what eats people' is kinda a big trope that's been done a lot, the angle of this being used by an authoritarian government is enough of a twist that this could still work. The problem here is that the narrative around that is very passive: there's no change since all that happens is like 'the Brazilian government used it and then they fell and now they don't': this feels like it needs a like, <a href="/scp-4511">SCP-4511</a> style descent where they start off putting small stuff in and then it's escalating demands forces them into more and more extreme behaviour. The downside of doing this is that as a commentary on real events, it begins to feel like it explains them as a necessary evil; and so you have to be very careful to ensure that this feels like a deliberate choice.</p>
<p>I think the note from the soldier encapsulates what I mean: having someone in a note plainly state &quot;What we are doing here is heinous. A blasphemy!&quot; is orders of magnitude less effectively in writing than <em>showing</em> us that it's heinous, showing us the blasphemy directly, y'know? Show us why they do it, show is why they keep doing it, show us how horrible it is.</p>

							</div>

								
					
